1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,300
6:15 in the morning.

2
00:00:01,860 --> 00:00:03,740
Your alarm goes off, and you tap the

3
00:00:03,740 --> 00:00:05,440
snooze button the way you always do.

4
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:08,260
The phone goes through the nightstand.

5
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:09,540
Then through the floor.

6
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:11,880
You hear it land in your downstairs neighbour's

7
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:12,960
kitchen, still ringing.

8
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:14,420
You sit up.

9
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:16,300
Something feels cold.

10
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:17,720
You touch your head.

11
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:19,480
There's nothing there.

12
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:21,000
Not a single hair.

13
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:24,940
Sometime during the night, you got the power

14
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:25,720
of Saitama.

15
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:27,260
One-Punch Man.

16
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:30,960
No transformation, no special moves, no gears, no

17
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:31,640
glowing aura.

18
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:33,440
Just this:

19
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:37,300
anything you hit with real intent goes down

20
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:37,940
in one punch.

21
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:39,280
Anything.

22
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:42,700
Within a few months you'll be the strongest

23
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:44,300
person who has ever lived.

24
00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:46,460
And you'll find out that the hardest thing

25
00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:48,460
about being unbeatable isn't the fighting.

26
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:50,400
It's everything after.

27
00:00:51,740 --> 00:00:52,480
First problem:

28
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:55,140
everything in your apartment is made of paper

29
00:00:55,140 --> 00:00:55,540
now.

30
00:00:55,540 --> 00:00:58,360
You squeeze the toothpaste and it empties in

31
00:00:58,360 --> 00:00:58,840
one shot.

32
00:00:59,380 --> 00:01:00,860
You turn the tap and it comes off

33
00:01:00,860 --> 00:01:01,480
in your hand.

34
00:01:02,340 --> 00:01:04,120
You pick up a mug and you're holding

35
00:01:04,120 --> 00:01:04,640
a handle.

36
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:08,620
So you learn rule one, the way Saitama

37
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:11,360
lives it: your strength doesn't turn off.

38
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:12,440
You do.

39
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:13,940
You move slowly.

40
00:01:14,620 --> 00:01:16,120
You hold things like they're eggs.

41
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:18,500
Door handles get one finger.

42
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:21,080
By lunchtime you can make a cup of

43
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:24,060
tea without destroying the kettle, and you have

44
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:25,700
never been prouder of anything.

45
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:26,800
And the hair.

46
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:29,400
Real stress hair loss takes two to four

47
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:31,460
months to show up, and it usually grows

48
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:31,880
back.

49
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:34,480
Yours took one night.

50
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:36,420
And it's not coming back.

51
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:39,480
You need to know how strong you are,

52
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:41,560
so you go to the quietest gym in

53
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:43,340
town and ask to use the heavy bag.

54
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:44,600
The trainer laughs.

55
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:46,020
You tap it.

56
00:01:46,020 --> 00:01:46,680
Barely.

57
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:48,980
The bag doesn't swing.

58
00:01:48,980 --> 00:01:52,500
It tears off the chain, goes through the

59
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:54,960
wall, and lands in the car park outside.

60
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:57,800
So you learn rule two.

61
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:00,780
With Saitama there are two kinds of punches:

62
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:03,620
the normal one, which is already too much,

63
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:07,040
and the serious one, which you never want

64
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:08,260
to find out about indoors.

65
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:10,800
You go home and practise holding back on

66
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:11,100
a pillow.

67
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:13,080
Then on a mattress.

68
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:15,560
Then you buy a new mattress.

69
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:19,240
On day five you test your speed somewhere

70
00:02:19,240 --> 00:02:22,100
safe: an empty field, nobody around.

71
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:23,400
You punch the air.

72
00:02:23,720 --> 00:02:24,540
Not at anything.

73
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:25,300
Just the air.

74
00:02:25,880 --> 00:02:28,420
There's a crack like a gunshot, and every

75
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:30,280
car alarm in the street behind you goes

76
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:30,680
off.

77
00:02:31,820 --> 00:02:33,940
The fastest punches ever measured in a lab

78
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:35,920
hit about 12 metres per second.

79
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:38,820
To make that crack, your fist broke the

80
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:41,920
sound barrier, around 343 metres per second.

81
00:02:42,180 --> 00:02:45,020
That's nearly thirty times faster, and about eight

82
00:02:45,020 --> 00:02:46,300
hundred times the energy.

83
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:49,360
You punched the sky, and the sky complained.

84
00:02:50,120 --> 00:02:51,340
Somebody filmed it from a balcony.

85
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:55,000
By day nine the video has twelve million

86
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:55,580
views.

87
00:02:56,340 --> 00:02:57,940
The comments are split right down the middle.

88
00:02:58,560 --> 00:02:59,660
Half of them say it's CGI.

89
00:03:00,340 --> 00:03:02,320
The other half say it's CGI, but good

90
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:02,740
CGI.

91
00:03:03,580 --> 00:03:04,340
Nobody believes you.

92
00:03:04,780 --> 00:03:05,840
That's the strange part.

93
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:08,180
You can stop a truck, but you can't

94
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:09,300
stop people saying you're fake.

95
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:12,000
You find out that being strong in real

96
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:13,500
life doesn't come with an audience.

97
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:16,200
It comes with a landlord asking who is

98
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:17,000
paying for the wall.

99
00:03:18,220 --> 00:03:20,180
On day twelve you try to live a

100
00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:20,780
normal life.

101
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:22,520
You have a job interview at nine.

102
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:24,940
The interviewer reaches out to shake your hand,

103
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:27,980
and you spend the longest second of your

104
00:03:27,980 --> 00:03:30,440
life deciding how hard a handshake is.

105
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:32,540
You go with "dead fish".

106
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:34,200
He winces anyway.

107
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:35,980
Then the coffee machine jams,

108
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:38,960
everyone sighs, and you give it a little

109
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:39,580
tap to help.

110
00:03:40,420 --> 00:03:40,940
It works again.

111
00:03:41,620 --> 00:03:43,300
It's also now part of the wall.

112
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:44,560
You don't get the job.

113
00:03:45,180 --> 00:03:46,860
On the way out, the security guard asks

114
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:47,440
for a selfie.

115
00:03:48,660 --> 00:03:50,840
On day seventeen you're walking home from the

116
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:53,040
supermarket when a crane on a building site

117
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:53,480
snaps.

118
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:56,120
A load of steel beams is falling toward

119
00:03:56,120 --> 00:03:57,780
a bus stop full of people.

120
00:03:58,680 --> 00:03:59,480
You don't think.

121
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:00,440
You jump.

122
00:04:01,160 --> 00:04:03,320
You catch the first beam, punch the second

123
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:05,760
one sideways into the empty lot, and set

124
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:07,480
the third one down on the pavement like

125
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:07,820
a sofa.

126
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:09,120
Nobody is hurt.

127
00:04:09,720 --> 00:04:11,420
It takes about four seconds.

128
00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:14,820
When the news arrives, a man in a

129
00:04:14,820 --> 00:04:17,340
shiny costume who arrived ten minutes later is

130
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:18,140
giving interviews.

131
00:04:18,620 --> 00:04:20,340
You're holding a bag of groceries with the

132
00:04:20,340 --> 00:04:21,440
eggs still intact.

133
00:04:22,140 --> 00:04:23,940
You decide that counts as a win.

134
00:04:25,080 --> 00:04:27,780
After the crane, your oldest friend shows up

135
00:04:27,780 --> 00:04:29,620
at your door at five in the morning

136
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:30,940
in running gear.

137
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:32,740
"Teach me," they say.

138
00:04:33,080 --> 00:04:33,500
"The routine.

139
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:36,420
A hundred push-ups, a hundred sit-ups, a hundred

140
00:04:36,420 --> 00:04:38,920
squats, ten kilometres.

141
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:39,400
Every day."

142
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:41,340
So you train together.

143
00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:44,680
And here's the thing: in real life, that

144
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:46,120
routine works,

145
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:47,300
for a while.

146
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:49,960
You get fitter fast in the first weeks,

147
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:51,800
then the progress slows down,

148
00:04:52,540 --> 00:04:54,740
and months of the same hundred push-ups is

149
00:04:54,740 --> 00:04:55,980
how a lot of people end up with

150
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:57,320
sore elbows and shoulders.

151
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:00,210
After three weeks your friend can run ten

152
00:05:00,210 --> 00:05:01,400
kilometres without stopping.

153
00:05:01,960 --> 00:05:02,400
They're proud.

154
00:05:02,940 --> 00:05:03,400
They should be.

155
00:05:03,940 --> 00:05:05,120
They still can't lift a car.

156
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:07,940
They ask what your secret really is.

157
00:05:08,580 --> 00:05:09,420
You honestly don't know.

158
00:05:10,780 --> 00:05:13,660
On day thirty, two people in grey suits

159
00:05:13,660 --> 00:05:14,500
knock on your door.

160
00:05:14,980 --> 00:05:15,440
Very polite.

161
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:17,640
They'd like to run a few tests.

162
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:20,580
They bring a machine that measures punching force.

163
00:05:21,240 --> 00:05:22,080
You hit it gently.

164
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:23,500
It stops working.

165
00:05:24,180 --> 00:05:25,080
They bring a bigger one.

166
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:26,320
Same thing.

167
00:05:26,740 --> 00:05:28,520
They bring a block of steel the size

168
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:31,260
of a fridge, and it folds like a

169
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:31,740
napkin.

170
00:05:32,620 --> 00:05:33,820
At the end of the day they don't

171
00:05:33,820 --> 00:05:34,440
have a number.

172
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:36,540
They have a report with one line:

173
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:38,700
"Upper limit: not found."

174
00:05:39,380 --> 00:05:40,200
And you notice something.

175
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:41,540
You're not nervous.

176
00:05:42,220 --> 00:05:43,280
You're not even curious.

177
00:05:44,260 --> 00:05:46,660
You're checking the time, because the supermarket has

178
00:05:46,660 --> 00:05:47,600
a sale at six.

179
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:49,740
Word gets around.

180
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:52,120
Fighters start showing up.

181
00:05:52,740 --> 00:05:53,800
Boxers, wrestlers,

182
00:05:54,540 --> 00:05:56,220
a man who has trained in the mountains

183
00:05:56,220 --> 00:05:57,500
for twelve years.

184
00:05:58,340 --> 00:05:59,780
Each of them gets one tap.

185
00:06:00,420 --> 00:06:02,340
You start apologising before you even swing.

186
00:06:03,160 --> 00:06:05,120
One of them asks you to fight seriously,

187
00:06:05,720 --> 00:06:06,260
just once.

188
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:07,520
You can't.

189
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:09,800
If you did, there would be no one

190
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:10,460
left to ask.

191
00:06:11,660 --> 00:06:14,080
By day sixty you've stopped trying to prove

192
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:14,480
anything.

193
00:06:14,860 --> 00:06:15,560
You just help.

194
00:06:16,100 --> 00:06:18,040
A car stuck in a flooded underpass at

195
00:06:18,040 --> 00:06:20,680
night: you carry it out, with the family

196
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:22,660
still inside, and set it down on dry

197
00:06:22,660 --> 00:06:22,900
road.

198
00:06:23,460 --> 00:06:25,260
A runaway shopping trolley heading for a busy

199
00:06:25,260 --> 00:06:26,600
street: one finger.

200
00:06:27,260 --> 00:06:29,900
A cat on a roof: you jump, very

201
00:06:29,900 --> 00:06:30,360
gently.

202
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:32,100
Nobody films those ones.

203
00:06:32,980 --> 00:06:34,020
Nobody writes about them.

204
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:36,420
A little kid in a window waves at

205
00:06:36,420 --> 00:06:37,880
you, and you wave back,

206
00:06:38,640 --> 00:06:39,600
and that's the whole reward.

207
00:06:40,440 --> 00:06:41,540
It should feel like enough.

208
00:06:42,300 --> 00:06:43,000
Some nights it does.

209
00:06:43,960 --> 00:06:46,200
This is the part nobody warns you about.

210
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:48,160
At first, winning felt amazing.

211
00:06:48,520 --> 00:06:49,580
Then it felt normal.

212
00:06:50,260 --> 00:06:51,440
Then it felt like nothing.

213
00:06:52,360 --> 00:06:54,460
Psychologists call it hedonic adaptation:

214
00:06:55,060 --> 00:06:57,280
we get used to almost anything, good or

215
00:06:57,280 --> 00:06:58,680
bad, and the thrill fades.

216
00:06:59,340 --> 00:07:01,700
Saitama's real enemy was never a monster.

217
00:07:02,340 --> 00:07:03,140
It was boredom.

218
00:07:04,820 --> 00:07:06,720
Every fight ends before it starts.

219
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:09,220
Every video game feels slow.

220
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:12,160
Every danger looks like it's standing still.

221
00:07:12,920 --> 00:07:15,120
You'd give anything for one opponent who makes

222
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:17,600
you sweat, just once, to remember what it

223
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:18,320
felt like to try.

224
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:21,900
You stop setting goals, because every goal is

225
00:07:21,900 --> 00:07:22,620
already done.

226
00:07:23,260 --> 00:07:25,380
You stop telling people about your day, because

227
00:07:25,380 --> 00:07:26,640
your day is always the same:

228
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:29,780
something happens, you hit it once, it's over.

229
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:33,020
The strongest person in the world, and the

230
00:07:33,020 --> 00:07:33,720
thing you miss most

231
00:07:34,400 --> 00:07:34,880
is losing.

232
00:07:36,180 --> 00:07:39,020
One night, just to feel something, you go

233
00:07:39,020 --> 00:07:40,940
to an empty beach and jump.

234
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:42,260
Not a careful jump.

235
00:07:42,620 --> 00:07:43,380
A real one.

236
00:07:43,380 --> 00:07:45,300
The ground cracks.

237
00:07:46,120 --> 00:07:47,840
The clouds go past like you're in an

238
00:07:47,840 --> 00:07:48,240
elevator.

239
00:07:48,780 --> 00:07:50,800
Then the sky turns black,

240
00:07:51,560 --> 00:07:53,560
and below you there's the curve of the

241
00:07:53,560 --> 00:07:53,740
Earth,

242
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:56,160
blue and white and silent.

243
00:07:56,880 --> 00:07:58,760
To leave Earth for good you'd need about

244
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:00,360
eleven kilometres per second.

245
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:02,040
You don't know if you went that fast.

246
00:08:02,780 --> 00:08:04,900
You know you're falling back, and for about

247
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:08,360
twenty seconds, falling feels like the most exciting

248
00:08:08,360 --> 00:08:10,460
thing that has happened to you in months.

249
00:08:11,300 --> 00:08:12,340
You land in the ocean.

250
00:08:12,340 --> 00:08:14,640
It takes you an hour to swim home.

251
00:08:15,260 --> 00:08:16,440
You smile the whole way.

252
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:20,660
And then, on day one hundred and twenty,

253
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:22,280
the sky turns red.

254
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,440
Something is coming in from space, a rock

255
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:28,360
the size of a stadium, and every government

256
00:08:28,360 --> 00:08:30,440
on Earth runs out of time at the

257
00:08:30,440 --> 00:08:31,220
same moment.

258
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:32,880
For the first time in months,

259
00:08:33,580 --> 00:08:34,580
your heart is pounding.

260
00:08:35,600 --> 00:08:37,980
Millions of people are looking up, and for

261
00:08:37,980 --> 00:08:39,520
once, so are you.

262
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:41,120
Not because you're scared.

263
00:08:41,120 --> 00:08:45,660
Because, finally, something is coming that might actually

264
00:08:45,660 --> 00:08:46,420
be a fight.

265
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:47,960
You jump.

266
00:08:48,780 --> 00:08:50,940
You meet it above the clouds, and for

267
00:08:50,940 --> 00:08:53,680
one second you throw a serious punch.

268
00:08:54,380 --> 00:08:56,840
The rock turns into gravel and dust.

269
00:08:57,720 --> 00:08:59,680
A few pieces still rain down on the

270
00:08:59,680 --> 00:08:59,880
city.

271
00:09:00,420 --> 00:09:02,720
A few roofs get holes, a few cars

272
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:04,100
get dents.

273
00:09:04,100 --> 00:09:04,780
Nobody dies.

274
00:09:05,480 --> 00:09:07,720
The next morning a headline asks who will

275
00:09:07,720 --> 00:09:08,600
pay for the damage.

276
00:09:08,600 --> 00:09:11,200
So you do what Saitama does.

277
00:09:12,080 --> 00:09:12,640
You go shopping.

278
00:09:13,760 --> 00:09:14,380
Eggs are half price,

279
00:09:15,280 --> 00:09:17,640
and for some reason that makes you happier

280
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:19,480
than saving the planet did.

281
00:09:20,440 --> 00:09:23,420
That night, you set your alarm for 6:15.

282
00:09:23,420 --> 00:09:25,500
In the morning it rings, and you reach

283
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:26,940
out and tap it.

284
00:09:26,940 --> 00:09:27,560
Gently.

285
00:09:28,260 --> 00:09:29,120
It stops ringing.

286
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:31,280
The nightstand is still there.

287
00:09:31,780 --> 00:09:33,620
Your friend texts: "Run tomorrow?

288
00:09:33,620 --> 00:09:35,980
Ten kilometres." You say yes.

289
00:09:36,560 --> 00:09:37,660
Not because you need it.

290
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:40,080
Because it's the one thing left that you

291
00:09:40,080 --> 00:09:41,240
get to do with someone else.

292
00:09:41,240 --> 00:09:44,000
It's the hardest thing you've learned all year.

293
00:09:45,300 --> 00:09:45,520
So:

294
00:09:46,180 --> 00:09:48,680
would you take one punch that wins every

295
00:09:48,680 --> 00:09:51,440
fight, if it meant nothing would ever feel

296
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:52,100
hard again?

297
00:09:53,020 --> 00:09:54,580
And what's the first thing you'd punch?

298
00:09:55,380 --> 00:09:56,060
Tell us in the comments.
